<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # First, I Cut the Paper. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 10px; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Following Instructions at School</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Cut the Paper.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Talk about simple rules and laws. Interactive: Answer questions about their daily routine.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe basic classroom rules related to school projects (e.g., "We must clean up after making the poster." "You have to wash your hands after using paint." "We should put away the scissors and glue when we're done."). Can answer and ask follow-up questions to classmates about steps in a class project as part of their routine (e.g., "What do I do after cutting the paper?" "Glue it on the poster." "Where do I put the markers after using them?" "In the supply box."). Can explain safety rules at school when using classroom materials (e.g., "You should use scissors carefully." "Always walk, don't run when carrying sharp objects.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows realia (paper, scissors, glue, crayons, a small poster) and asks students: "What is this?" (pointing to scissors) "What do we do with paper?" "What can we make?" The teacher asks students about rules for using these items. "What must we do after using scissors?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up classroom materials one by one and models simple instructions and rules. "First, I cut the paper. Next, I paste the paper on the poster. We must clean up after art class." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /p/ in "paper" and "poster", and /s/ in "scissors", asking students to repeat. The teacher also models dividing "classroom" into "class-room" (two-syllables).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and gestures to check understanding. "Do we run with scissors? (No!)" (teacher shakes head). "Do we put glue on the table? (No!)" (teacher points to a trash can). "What do we do with the paper when we are finished?" (expecting "put it in the trash" or "recycle it").</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5–7 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated sequence of instructions on the board or projected, showing steps for making a simple mask or a decorative card. The instructions include imperatives, sequencing words, and classroom rules.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example:
                <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. First, draw a big circle on the paper.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Next, cut the circle carefully with scissors.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Then, decorate your mask with crayons or paint.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Finally, let's clean up our space. Put the scissors away.</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understand and respond to simple instructions and questions about daily routines, especially related to school projects and classroom rules.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the instructions aloud, pointing to the pictures. Students listen and repeat chorally.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are we going to make?" (A mask/card). "Are these instructions for home or for school?" (For school/classroom).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sequencing activity:</b> Students point to the picture that shows "First...", "Next...", "Then...", "Finally...".</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7–10 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using plural imperatives ("Let's make a poster."), sequencing words ("first, next, then, finally"), and future tense ("I will make a mask." "I am going to draw a picture.") to discuss rules and steps for projects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion & Choral Repetition.</b> The teacher writes incomplete sentences on the board (e.g., "First, ___ the paper." "Next, ___ it on the poster." "Let's ___ our supplies."). Students orally complete the sentences using target verbs (cut, paste, put away). The class repeats the full sentences chorally, focusing on pronunciation of /p/ and /s/.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Chain Drill.</b> Students stand in a circle. The teacher starts with an instruction: "First, draw a flower." The next student says, "First, draw a flower. Next, cut the flower." The next student adds another step using sequencing words and an imperative. This continues around the circle, building a sequence of instructions. The teacher guides to ensure correct grammar and pronunciation.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: Give Me Instructions.</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards showing simple steps of an activity (e.g., making a paper boat, drawing a house, cleaning a desk). Student A arranges the cards in order and gives instructions to Student B using "First, Next, Then, Finally." Student B listens and can ask "What do I need to do next?" Student A also practices saying "I will..." or "We are going to..." for future intentions.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15–18 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Classroom Project Role-Play.</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group chooses a simple classroom art project (e.g., "Making a mask for a school play," "Decorating a classroom poster," "Creating a big card for the teacher").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses and decides on 3-4 steps for their chosen project, incorporating materials like paper, scissors, glue, paint, crayons, etc.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student acts as the "teacher" or "leader" who gives instructions using sequencing words (First, Next, Then, Finally) and imperatives (Cut the paper, Paste it, Decorate it).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other students act as classmates. They listen, follow instructions verbally (pretending to do the actions), and actively ask for clarification: "What do I need to do next?" or "Where do I put the scissors?" They also state future intentions: "I will draw a big flower." or "We are going to use red paint."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups also include one safety rule (e.g., "Use scissors carefully. Always walk, don't run.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups present their "project planning" role-play to another group or the whole class.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in giving and following multi-step instructions, using target vocabulary, sequencing words, plural imperatives, asking for clarification, and communicating future intentions within the scenario "Following Instructions at School" and theme "First, I Cut the Paper."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10–12 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe students' speaking during the role-play, focusing on specific criteria.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Classroom Project Role-Play" in their groups.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
            <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses sequencing words (first, next, then, finally).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses imperatives correctly (cut, paste, draw, clean up).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asks and answers clarification questions ("What do I need to do next?").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Communicates future intentions ("I will make a mask.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces /p/ and /s/ clearly.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates well with group members.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket individually.</p>
            <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. One new instruction I can give is: "_______."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. One question I can ask to understand better is: "_______."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
            <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What was easy/difficult when giving instructions to my classmates?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. How did I help my group during the project role-play?</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in promoting speaking and interaction in this lesson? (e.g., specific activities, grouping, materials).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with sequencing or asking for clarification? (e.g., more sentence frames, visual aids).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will practice writing down simple instructions and classroom rules, consolidating the vocabulary and grammatical structures learned in this speaking lesson. This will lead to creating a short set of instructions for a class project.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>